<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
    Haitian Creole (Kreyòl ayisyen), a draft not yet read by a fluent reviewer (#495). The notes on each string are in
    values/strings.xml. Standard IPN spelling, everyday spoken register, "ou" for you.
    "Vehicle" is machin, the everyday word for any road vehicle (veyikil reads as officialese), never the word for bus;
    "route" is wout; "run" is kous, a driver's word for one run of the route; "trip" is vwayaj; "stop" is arè;
    "block" is blòk; "agency" is konpayi; "dispatcher" is sant kontwòl (the control room); "report" is rapò;
    "riders" is pasaje; "sign in" and "sign out" are konekte and dekonekte, so a network connection is always entènèt
    (or rezo for signal), never koneksyon; "timetable" and "schedule" are both orè; "location" is pozisyon, as French
    Android says Position; "resume" is repran; "tap" is peze; "status card" is bwat estati, since kat is the map;
    "diagnostics" is enfòmasyon teknik.
    Plurals: CLDR has no Haitian Creole rules, so Android always picks "other"; each plural has that one form,
    worded to read right for any count.
-->
